==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|grc|en}} {{term|διδακτικός||skilled in teaching|tr=didaktikos|lang=grc|sc=polytonic}} from {{term|διδακτός||taught, learnt|tr=didaktos|lang=grc|sc=polytonic}} from {{term|διδάσκω||I teach, educate|tr=didaskō|lang=grc|sc=polytonic}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|dī-dăkˈtĭk}}, {{IPA|/daɪˈdæk.tɪk/}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{archaic}} A [[treatise]] on teaching or [[education]].

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Instructive or intended to [[teach]] or [[demonstrate]], especially with regard to [[morality]]. (I.e., didactic [[poetry]])
# Excessively moralizing.
# Regarding medicine, teaching from [[textbook]]s rather than laboratory demonstration and clinical application.

